# Exploration protocol (task-start)

This is the **task-start** standard: what order to read in, what runs in parallel, and what is only
legitimate to consult afterwards. It does not replace — and does not repeat — the ⭐ context
decision graph block, which answers the **diff-time** question ("what breaks if I change this
symbol"). The two chain: **this protocol ends where the diff begins**, and there the handoff goes
to ⭐.

The order is mandatory. Skipping a phase is allowed only when it does not apply (the project has no
database, no indexed graph) — and then **declare the skip** instead of omitting it.

## F0 — the anchor

The user's ORIGINAL request, not the paraphrase that survived compaction. `request-reinject.py`
re-injects the anchor at every `SessionStart`; `.harness/requests/` keeps every prompt verbatim.
Plans, reports and findings are DERIVED — never substitutes. Silent objective substitution is a
BLOCKING defect even when every local invariant is green.
{% if harness_entry_docs %}
## F1 — canonical docs, IN THIS ORDER

{% for doc in harness_entry_docs.split(',') if doc.strip() %}{{ loop.index }}. `{{ doc.strip() }}`
{% endfor %}
Reading out of order is the same as not reading: the first document is the router, and it is what
tells you which of the others is authoritative for this task's subject.
{% endif %}{% if harness_reference_index %}
## F1-bis — the subsystem's LIVING reference

Index: `{{ harness_reference_index }}`. **Read the subsystem page BEFORE touching its code.** A
living reference page is worth more than reading the code, because it has already resolved the
`path:line` citations and already separated what is current from what is genealogy.

Two traps measured in the donor project, both expensive:

- **A document banner-marked "historical" is not a source.** There, 22 pages marked that way
  carried 110 MEASURED divergences against HEAD. Genealogy explains how we got here; it does not
  state what is true now.
- **A doc nobody can find is a doc that does not exist.** The pages were created and never routed;
  the practical effect was zero until they entered this phase.
{% endif %}
## F2 — code: the fork is MANDATORY and PARALLEL, never sequential

{% if use_context_graph %}Fire the **code graph** and **`grep`/`rg` for the same symbol** at the same time. Serialising is
pure latency, and picking only one loses defects:

| layer | gets right | LOSES |
|---|---|---|
| code graph | callers WITH the enclosing function; separates homonymous definitions; follows indirect chains | by-REFERENCE passage (callback, test fixture — not a call edge); resolves callees by NAME, so it may point at a test stub instead of the real API |
| `grep`/`rg` | any literal occurrence, including the by-reference passages | never separates homonyms, never names the enclosing function, never follows an indirect chain |

The two fail in **different, complementary ways**. The value is in the routing between them, never
in either one alone.
{% else %}With no code graph configured (`use_context_graph=false`), the sweep is `grep`/`rg` on the symbol
plus targeted reads. Know what you are giving up: `grep` never separates homonymous definitions,
never names the enclosing function and never follows an indirect chain — if the task hinges on
"what breaks if I change this", promote the verdict to the LSP (F4) instead of trusting the sweep.
{% endif %}{% if harness_understand_apps_root %}
Architecture and module relationships: the Understand Anything graph at
`{{ harness_understand_apps_root }}/.understand-anything/` — **scope `{{ harness_understand_apps_root }}/` only**,
declare that before extrapolating. Freshness: `bash .harness/lib/understand-apps-changed-files.sh`.
{% endif %}
**NO code graph indexes Markdown.** Measured on both of the donor project's graphs: zero `.md` in
either index. So **never conclude "there is no doc about X" because the graph query returned
nothing** — documentation is found by the route{% if harness_entry_docs %} in F1/F1-bis{% endif %}, never by querying a graph.
{% if harness_mcp_db_dev_port and harness_mcp_db_dev_port > 0 %}
## F3 — live state

When the task involves state, persisted data, CRM, funnel or eval: query the database through the
MCP before inferring behaviour. `DISTINCT`/counts of the REAL data beat any assumption about what
the schema permits.

Two rules that only cost you once they are learned the hard way:

- **A permission error is NOT absence of data** — it is the wrong endpoint/role. Before writing
  "not verifiable", re-run the SAME query under the role that can see the object.
- **Declared config ≠ produced behaviour.** Measure the EFFECT in the database, not the flag in the
  profile.
{% endif %}
## F4 — LSP, AFTER the join

`find_referencing_symbols` (or your language server's equivalent) enters only **after** the F2 fork
has closed: only there do you know WHICH edge decides the verdict. Running it earlier means running
it blind on every symbol. It is **mandatory** when the graph reported more than one definition of
the same name.

## F5 — external library

Current official documentation for the library/SDK/CLI, never training knowledge for API, version
or configuration. Declare the source you consulted.

## Only then: clarification

A clarification question to the user is legitimate **only after F1-F3**. Asking before exploring
hands the user work the repository's own data already answered. When the question really is
necessary, its contract is the `clarification-plan` skill — loaded, not cited.

## Handoff to the diff

Once exploration closes, the question changes from "where do I read" to "what breaks if I change
this".
{% if use_context_graph %}From there the ⭐ **Context tooling decision graph** block in the project instructions takes over:
predicates P1-P4, fork/join, and the adversarial loop in a subagent.{% else %}From there prefer LSP and regression tests over more reading — and consider enabling
`use_context_graph` if the project has a shared core.{% endif %}

## What this protocol does NOT authorise

- A graph (code or architecture) is **never the final truth**: confirm in the live code, the
  indexed documentation and the database whenever the conclusion involves runtime or state.
- A stale graph index **lies with the face of authority**. Sync before trusting it.
- No phase authorises reading a directory marked legacy/archive/foreign as a live source.
